  "note": "🎚️ The number-control laws every renderer shares: the slider detent law (`snaps` finite, strictly ascending, inside min..=max), the pointer law (clamp, quantize onto the step ladder from min, then pull onto the nearest snap within 3 % of the span, first snap wins a tie), the keyboard law of sliders and number fields (arrows walk the step ladder from min — from 0 without one —, a large arrow ten rungs, and never snap; from an off-ladder detent the first rung beyond it is one rung; page keys jump to the next detent strictly above or below, else ten rungs; Home and End reach the bounds and keep the value without one; an invalid step walks rungs of one; results clamp to the bounds and clean to the decimals of min and step), and the fixed-precision law (the spoken value of a number field or stepper carrying a precision, and exactly `precision` fraction digits, ties away from zero as JavaScript's toFixed at every magnitude, unsigned zero, precision capped at 15, magnitudes from 1e21 fall back to the twelve-digit format). Answered by `snaps_are_valid`/`slider_pointer_value`/`slider_adjacent_snap`/`slider_key_value`/`format_ui_number_fixed`/`round_ui_number` in Rust and their camelCase twins in TypeScript; decimal.js is the third-party rounding oracle.",
